You can use the Scanner class to read input from the user. Then, you can use a switch statement to handle the different options.
Here is an example code:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Game {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Question: What do you want to do?");
        System.out.println("1. Option 1");
        System.out.println("2. Option 2");
        System.out.println("3. Option 3");

        int choice = scanner.nextInt();

        switch (choice) {
            case 1:
                System.out.println("You chose Option 1.");
                break;
            case 2:
                System.out.println("You chose Option 2.");
                break;
            case 3:
                System.out.println("You chose Option 3.");
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid choice.");
                break;
        }
    }
}
In this example, the program first prints the question and the options. Then, it reads the user's input using the Scanner class.

